(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027-106"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027-106","title":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Theory of Computation | Topic Wise Test 1","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Theory of Computation | Topic Wise Test 1","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027","num":106,"category":"topicwise","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2700,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Theory of Computation"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027/070_exam_1367_GO Classes CS Test Series Theory of Computation Topic Wise Test 1.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Note the following abbreviations:</p><p>$\\text{DFA}=$ Deterministic Finite Automata $\\text{NFA}&nbsp;=$ Nondeterministic Finite Automata.</p><p>Which of the following is/are False?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>A DFA is equivalent in expressive power to an NFA.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>A finite automaton cannot recognize any infinite language.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The language $\\left\\{0^{n} 0^{n} \\mid n&gt;=0\\right\\}$ is regular.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>For every NFA with $n$ states, there is an equivalent DFA with exactly $2^{n}$ states.</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"376573\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution of Question 1:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1MiJGnZluLs&amp;t=82s\" target=\"_blank\">Question 1 - Detailed Video Solution (Click Here)</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>The language $\\left\\{0^{n} 0^{n} \\mid n\\geq 0\\right\\}$ is regular because it is same as $\\left\\{0^{2 n} \\mid n\\geq 0\\right\\}$, which is also an infinite language, so option B is false.<br><br>D is true.<br><br>The following statement is Not true: \"For every NFA with $n$ states, there is an equivalent <strong>minimal DFA</strong> with exactly $2^{n}$ states.\"</p><p><strong>Helpful Point&nbsp;1:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376572/classes-test-series-2023-theory-computation-test-question?show=376583#c376583\" target=\"_blank\">HERE.</a>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Helpful Point 2:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376572/classes-test-series-2023-theory-computation-test-question?show=376964#a376964\" target=\"_blank\">HERE.</a>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 21)</small></h4><a name=\"376964\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>I think the confusion is for&nbsp;Option D right !.</p>\n\n<p>So, let me try explaining why it is true :&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>“<strong>For every NFA with&nbsp;n&nbsp;states, there is an equivalent DFA with exactly&nbsp;2^n&nbsp;states.</strong>”</p>\n\n<p>this is true because: “with n state NFA we can have <strong>at most</strong> 2^n states <strong>Minimal</strong> DFA”</p>\n\n<p>If we don't care about minimal at all then we can make any DFA&nbsp;having&nbsp;even more than 2^n states.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"408781\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The question is asking which of the statements is/are FALSE?</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\" type=\"A\">\n\t<li>True Statement</li>\n\t<li>False Statement [ L=(a+b)* , is an infinite language, we can design NFA / DFA . option B they are saying FA cannot recognize any infinite language ]</li>\n\t<li>True Statement&nbsp;</li>\n\t<li>True Statement [ Number of states in mDFA is at most 2^n, the option is not talking about mDFA, so we can design DFA, let’s say if the number of states is not equal to 2^n after designing it, we can add some unreachable states in DFA to make it 2^n ]</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>So the Final answer is B</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The minimal deterministic finite automata for the language $\\mathrm{L}=\\left\\{1^{m}&nbsp; \\mid \\mathrm{m} \\geq 0\\right.$ and $\\left.\\mathrm{m} \\neq 3\\right\\}$ over alphabet $\\Sigma=\\{0,1\\}$ will have</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$5$ states and $4$ final states</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$6$ states and $5$ final states</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$3$ states and $1$ final states</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$6$ states and $4$ final states</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 39)</small></h4><a name=\"409809\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Alphabet $\\sum = \\{0,1\\}$ &amp; Language $L = \\{1^m&nbsp; \\;\\;&nbsp;| \\;\\; m \\geq 0 \\;\\; and \\;\\; m \\neq 3\\}$</p>\n\n<p>So, the language $L$ contains $\\{\\epsilon, 1, 11, 1111, 11111, …..\\}$ $\\text{Remember we should not accept 111 as it is given m $\\neq$&nbsp;3}$</p>\n\n<p>$\\color{DarkGreen} DFA:$</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35f9d0fdd0b22f110caf.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p>So, In above DFA we have $6$ states and $4$ final states.</p>\n\n<p>$Ans: D$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 17)</small></h4><a name=\"376569\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:</strong>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;</a></p><p>The minimal DFA will have $6$ states, and $4$ final states. There is one dead state.<br><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"352\" width=\"624\" src=\"media/31/31113c9f4b34e1dd1bd6.jpg\"></strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376568\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Number of states in the minimal DFA which accepts the language over the alphabet $\\Sigma=\\{\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}\\}$ such that the language consists of all strings that contain at least one occurrence of each symbol in $\\Sigma$.","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 37)</small></h4><a name=\"376567\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:</strong>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;&nbsp;\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;&nbsp;</a></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"389\" src=\"media/dc/dc27daf7434c906c17fb.png\" width=\"624\"></strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"433723\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"658\" src=\"media/1a/1ab0f88c6586f07beb0d.jpg\">total states required : 4</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376566\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{M}$ be a finite automaton and let $\\text{M}^{\\prime}$ be obtained from $\\text{M}$ by interchanging the collections of accepting and non-accepting states. i.e. If $\\text{Q}$ is the set of states&nbsp;in $\\text{M}$ and $\\text{F}$ is the set of accepting states in $\\text{M}$ then $\\text{Q} \\backslash&nbsp;\\text{F}$ is the set of accepting states in $\\text{M}’.$<br>Now consider the following statements :</p><ol><li>If $\\text{M}$ is deterministic, then the language accepted by $\\text{M}^{\\prime}$ is the complement of the language accepted by $\\text{M} .$</li><li>If $\\text{M}$ is Non-deterministic, then the language accepted by $\\text{M}^{\\prime}$ is the complement of the language accepted by $\\text{M} .$</li></ol><p>Which of the above is/are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Only $1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only $2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Both</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 15)</small></h4><a name=\"376557\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$\\color{red}{\\text{Detailed Video Solution, With Proof:}}$</p><p><strong>MUST Watch this for Statement 2:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtu.be/jpciKGP4gT4?feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/jpciKGP4gT4?feature=shared</a>&nbsp;</p><p>Statement 1 Proof:&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtu.be/HE2iPU8qZaQ\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/HE2iPU8qZaQ</a>&nbsp;</p><p>If $M$ is deterministic, then the language accepted by $M^{\\prime}$ is the complement of the language accepted by $\\mathrm{M}.$<br><br>If $\\mathrm{M}$ is Non-deterministic, then the language accepted by $M^{\\prime}$ may not be the complement of the language accepted by $M$ because NFA might have dead configurations or/and non-determinism.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"409770\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$ Answer$ : A</p>\n\n<p>$Statement$ $2$ : Counterexample,&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"96\" src=\"media/4f/4f3f4099ce1f3305c6c3.jpg\" width=\"337\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376556\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{L}_1$ be the language $\\{a,aa,aaa \\}$ and let $\\text{L}_2$ be the language of all strings not in $\\{a, aa, aaa \\}$. Let the number of states in the minimal DFA that accepts $\\text{L}_1$ and $\\text{L}_2$ be '$m$' and '$n$' respectively. Then which of the following is correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$m&gt;n$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$n=m$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$m &lt;n$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Can&#x27;t say anything about $m$ and $n$.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"376555\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution &amp; GATE PYQ:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3szxKVVUo1A\" target=\"_blank\">Detailed Video Solution, with Complete Analysis &amp; Proof</a>&nbsp;</p><p>The number of states in the minimal DFA that accepts a regular language &nbsp;$L$ is same as the number of states in the minimal DFA that accepts complement of $L.$ (Learn Proof in the above video solution)<br><br>So, $m = n.$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"382082\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Here, in this qn DFA given , so number of states in minimal DFA &nbsp;and number of states in its complement is same only, here in minimal DFA, by just complementing no. of final states we will get complement<br>\n<br>\nNOTE:-<br>\n<br>\nBut in case of NFA no. of states in a language and its complement may/may not be same !</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"447576\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"img00\" width=\"1200\" height=\"384\" src=\"media/a9/a9be14beb6c9b37de764.jpg\"></p><p>L1 : a + aa + aaa</p><p>L2 : ε + a.a.a.a^+</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"411938\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>L 2 is complement of&nbsp;language L1</p>\n\n<p>In DFA&nbsp;there are unique transition for each symbol so it can be easily proved&nbsp;that because of determinism in DFA, states must be same i. e<strong>&nbsp; <em>m = n</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>But in case of NFA, it need not to be true always. Due to non determinism property in NFA&nbsp;and missing transition it cannot accept complement of language by just interchanging the final states to non final and vice versa</p>\n\n<p>So we cannot make any statement about number of states in complement of language in NFA.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"412429\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Number of states in DFA and its complement are Same.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":6,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{M}=(\\mathrm{Q}, \\Sigma, \\delta, q0, \\mathrm{~F})$ be a DFA. If $\\mathrm{F}$ contains at least one state, the language of $\\text{M}$ must be nonempty.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Let $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ be regular languages. Let $\\text{A-B}$ consist of all the strings in $\\text{A}$ which are not in $\\text{B}$ (this is sometimes written $\\text{A} \\backslash \\text{B}$ and is also called the set difference). Then $\\text{A-B}$ is regular.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Let $\\text{M}=(\\mathrm{Q}, \\Sigma, \\delta, q0, \\text{F})$ be a DFA. If $F$ is empty (i.e. No final state), the language of $\\text{M}$ must be empty.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{M}=(\\mathrm{Q}, \\Sigma, \\delta,q0, \\mathrm{~F})$ be a DFA. If $\\mathrm{F}$ contains $q0,$&nbsp;the language of $\\mathrm{M}$ must be nonempty.</p>"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 30)</small></h4><a name=\"412646\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Option A ). It is False because having a final state in DFA does not guarantee that language must be non-empty. Because if that final state is unreachable from the initial state, the language is empty. <br>\n<br>\nBut instead of DFA if it is minimal DFA then this option must be true. Because in Minimal DFA unreachable states are not allowed. Language must be nonempty if the final state is there in minimal DFA.<br>\n<br>\nOption B). It is TRUE, As A and B are regular already given, then using Product Construction we can have A – B DFA also so if we have DFA then the language must be regular.<br>\n<br>\nOption C). It is trivially true. No final state means the language is empty.<br>\n<br>\nOption D). It is also true. Because the initial state is a final state there are two reasons of the language cannot be non-empty.<br>\n<br>\n1). Since the initial is final then the final is reachable from the initial.<br>\n<br>\n2). An empty string must belong to the language iff the initial state is a final state in the case of DFA. so the language is non-empty. <br>\n<br>\nThe answer is B, C, D.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"376563\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>If all final states are unreachable then $\\text{L}$ is empty. If $\\text{F}$ contains $q0,$ the language of $\\text{M}$ must be nonempty because at least empty string will be in $\\text{L}.$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1MiJGnZluLs&amp;t=4161s\" target=\"_blank\">Detailed Video Solution (Click HERE)</a></strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376562\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following NFA(Nondeterministic Finite Automata) $N$ over alphabet $\\Sigma = \\{a, b, c\\},$ recognizing the language $L(N):$<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"189\" src=\"media/a5/a517356c1edd73d955af.png\" width=\"452\"></strong><br>\nWhat will be the number of states in the minimal DFA(Deterministic Finite Automata) for $L(N) ?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 17)</small></h4><a name=\"376561\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:</strong>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;</a></p>\n\n<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"353\" src=\"media/e4/e45e04f0df0233e33bd2.png\" width=\"624\"></strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"434398\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Check it out, do let me know any correction.</p><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"634\" height=\"314\" src=\"media/51/517f179c5db8fa7929d1.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"435205\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>&nbsp;</p><p>lower image is to check whether the dfa can be further minimized or not(which in this case can't be further minimized)</p><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"899\" height=\"1599\" src=\"media/41/41729842dc00dbd8ac2a.jpg\"></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376560\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following is the Language accepted by this automata?<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"199\" src=\"media/5b/5b27ca9d6d12d4b17fb8.png\" width=\"624\"></strong></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Set of all strings ending with $a.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Set of strings with at least one $a$ and an even number of $b’s.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Set of strings with at least one $a$ and an even number of $b’s$ following the last $a.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Set of strings ending with $a$ and an even number of $b’s$ following the last $a.$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 20)</small></h4><a name=\"376559\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1MiJGnZluLs&amp;t=5538s\" target=\"_blank\">Detailed Video Solution (Click HERE)</a></strong></p>\n\n<p>Strings with at least one $a$ and an even number of $b’s$ following the last $a.$<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"195\" src=\"media/f8/f8afd4de199d8ce1c2fd.png\" width=\"624\"></strong></p>\n\n<p>Option C is correct answer.</p>\n\n<p>NOTE that the language contains those strings which contain&nbsp;at least one $a$ and an even number of $b’s$ <strong>after&nbsp;the last</strong> $a.$</p>\n\n<p>Strings like $abb,aba$ are accepted, because they contain at least one $a$ and have even number of $b’s$ <strong>after the last occurrence of</strong> $a.$</p>\n\n<p>Helpful Comment:&nbsp;<a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376558/classes-test-series-2023-theory-computation-test-question?show=388484#c388484\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">HERE</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1MiJGnZluLs&amp;t=5538s\" target=\"_blank\">Detailed Video Solution (Click HERE)</a></strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376558\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $M$ be the Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) shown below:</p>\n\n<p><br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"288\" src=\"media/2a/2af54b7a140466f57be4.png\" width=\"536\"></strong></p>\n\n<p>The DFA shown above describes a context-free language $L$ over input alphabet $= \\{0, 1\\}.$ How many strings of length $10$ over $\\{0,1\\}$ do not belong to $L?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1024,1024],"answerText":"1024","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 72)</small></h4><a name=\"376553\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:</strong>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;</a></p>\n\n<p>The DFA shown above describes a language over $\\{0,1\\}$ that contains strings with exactly $2\\; 0's.$<br>\n<br>\nFor length $10,$ we have a total $2^{(10)}=1024$ strings, out of which $10 \\mathrm{C} 2= 45$ strings have exactly $2$ zeros. Hence, the answer is $1024-45=979$.<br>\n<br>\nNote that Because the language $L$ is defined by a DFA $M,$ it is a regular language. A regular language is always a context free language. That is because a CFL is defined by a PDA and a PDA is equivalent to a NFA with a stack-so it includes all NFA's.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 13)</small></h4><a name=\"376965\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>This is how I approach this question :&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>If you observe carefully, the pattern is :&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>[111…….x times] <strong>0</strong> [111…...y times] <strong>0</strong> [11…..z times]</p>\n\n<p>Now total length should be 10 so, x + 1 + y + 1 + z = 10 : x + y + z = 8.</p>\n\n<p>Now to solve x + y + z = 8.</p>\n\n<p>I take first :</p>\n\n<p>x = 8 →&nbsp;&nbsp;y = 0 and z = 0 [1 way]</p>\n\n<p>x = 7 →&nbsp;y = 1 and&nbsp; z = 0&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; y = 0 and z = 1 [2 ways]</p>\n\n<p>…...similarly pattern is :&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>8 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0 (x value)</p>\n\n<p>1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 (ways) = 9*10/2 = 45&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>So, U&nbsp; –&nbsp;45 = answer</p>\n\n<p>U = 2^10 = 1024</p>\n\n<p>so, 1024 –&nbsp;45 = <strong>979.</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Thank you.</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"415161\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Here it would be difficult to recognize all those strings not belongs to L <br>\nSo will Go with complement Approach <br>\nTotal strings can be formed with 2^10 ways as {0,1} there .<br>\nNow here the language is all those strings with exactly two 0’s <br>\nSo for 10 length strings , two 0 can be anywhere it will be 10C2 choice and rest 1’s have only 1 choice <br>\nNow , Required Ans is Total – &nbsp;10c2 ==&gt; 1024 -45 =979</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"486632\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>L = { w in (0+1)* | w has exact two zeros }&nbsp;<br><br>Total possible pattern of length 10 is 2^10 = 1024&nbsp;</p><p><br>With context of&nbsp; language L we have 2 zeros and 8 ones&nbsp;</p><p>= 10! / 2! * 8!&nbsp;</p><p>= 45&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h3>Ans : 1024 - 45&nbsp; = <strong>979&nbsp;</strong></h3></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"497510\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>_ 0 _ 0 _ = 10</strong></p><p><strong>10-2 = 8.&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>if we take 8 once in first _ : 1 possibilty .</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 7 = 2&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 6 = 3&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 5 = 4</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 4 = 5</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 3 = 6&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 2 = 7</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 1 = 8</strong></p><p><strong>\" \" 0 = 9</strong></p><p><strong>1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8+9 = 9(9+1)/2 = 45 .&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>number non accepted strings of length 10 = total number of strings possible - strings accepted of length 10 .&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>total string possible of length 10 = 2^10 = 1024&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>1024 - 45 = 979 .&nbsp;</strong></p><p><strong>979 is answer .&nbsp;</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"503562\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>&nbsp;</p><p><a id=\"gdev-id-503561\" title=\"\"></a></p><p>ans=total - num of string with excetly 2 '0' (of length 10)<br>1024-10c2=1024-45=979</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376552\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The following statement is known as \"the pumping lemma for regular language\":</p>\n\n<p>If $\\mathrm{A}$ is a regular language, then there is a number $p$ (the pumping length) where, if $s$ is any string in $\\text{A}$ of length at least $p$, then $s$ can be divided into $3$ pieces, $s&nbsp;= xyz$, satisfying the following conditions:</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>For each $i&nbsp;\\geq 0, x y^{i} z \\in \\text{A},$</li>\n\t<li>$|y|&gt;0$, and</li>\n\t<li>$|x y| \\leq p$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Your friend John is trying to prove that the language $w w^{R}$, the language of even length palindromes over $\\{0,1\\}$, is not regular.<br>\nFor pumping length $p$ he chooses the string $\\mathrm{S}$. Which of the following strings, can he use for the pumping lemma for regular languages to prove that this language is not regular?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}=01^{p} 1^{p} 0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}=1^{p} 01^{p}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}=0^{p} 1^{p} 1^{p} 0^{p}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}=0^{p} 1^{p} 0^{p} 1^{p}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 20)</small></h4><a name=\"376551\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Detailed Video Solution:</strong>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/1MiJGnZluLs&nbsp;</a></p>\n\n<p><strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">Pumping Lemma Complete Playlist:</a></strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared</a>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>String in option B,D doesn't belong to the language $w w^{R}$.<br>\nOption A:<br>\nAlthough the language is not regular, you can find an assignment of xyz that can be pumped. Try the<br>\nfollowing:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\mathrm{x}=0, \\mathrm{y}=11, \\mathrm{z}=1^{p-2} 1^{p} 0<br>\n$$<br>\nWhen we pump it, we get something of the form $01^{i} 0$, where $i\\geq 2 p -2$ and $i$ is even. This is always a even length palindrome.<br>\nNote that if you choose $1^{p} 01^{p}$ then you cannot pump it.</p>\n\n<p>The answer is Only Option C.</p>\n\n<hr>\n<p>$\\color{Red}{\\text{Understand Complete Pumping Lemma, Crystal Clear:}}$</p>\n\n<p>Here is&nbsp;&nbsp;<strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">Pumping Lemma Complete Playlist:</a></strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared</a>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>This Pumping Lemma playlist&nbsp;contains <strong>EVERYTHING about Pumping Lemma</strong> of Regular Languages, i.e. Proof, Examples, Variations, GATE PYQs, Finding minimum pumping length etc. Watch this playlist&nbsp;for Complete, Correct &amp; Clear Understanding.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"506483\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Eveyone Knows C option is correct.<br>\nSo let's comes to confusion why option A is not correct.<br>\nlet's suppose p =2 then 011110<br>\nSo, those who only seen the first or second lect. of Deepak sir they will think it is correct but it is not ,because y can exists any where in the string.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376550\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following statements :</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>If a deterministic finite automaton $\\text{M}$ accepts at least one string, then it accepts at least one string whose length is less than the number of states in $\\text{M}.$</li>\n\t<li>If a non-deterministic finite automaton $\\text{M}$ accepts at least one string, then it accepts at least one string whose length is less than the number of states in $\\text{M}.$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the above statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Only $1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only $2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Both</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 33)</small></h4><a name=\"376545\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Assume that a FA(DFA or NFA) accepts a string w of length greater than or equal to $n,$ where $n$ is the number of states in that FA, then to process the first $n$ symbols of this string, the FA has to go through a sequence of at least &nbsp;$n+1$ states but there are only $n$ distinct states so &nbsp;by pigeon hole principle, some state must repeat. So, we can remove that repetition and get a smaller string which belongs to our language. Hence, we would have a string in the language which has length less than the number of states in FA. So both statements are true.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"539216\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><section dir=\"auto\" data-turn-id=\"request-WEB:13f30df6-126f-4a99-8a78-7291979cff74-1\" data-turn-id-container=\"request-WEB:13f30df6-126f-4a99-8a78-7291979cff74-1\" data-testid=\"conversation-turn-4\" data-turn=\"assistant\"><h3 data-section-id=\"yrmhbk\" data-start=\"49\" data-end=\"70\">Statement 1 (DFA)</h3><p data-start=\"72\" data-end=\"103\">Suppose a DFA has <strong data-start=\"90\" data-end=\"102\">5 states</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"105\" data-end=\"124\">The statement says:</p><blockquote data-start=\"126\" data-end=\"229\"><p data-start=\"128\" data-end=\"229\">If the DFA accepts <strong data-start=\"147\" data-end=\"154\">any</strong> string, then it must accept <strong data-start=\"183\" data-end=\"228\">at least one string of length less than 5</strong>.</p></blockquote><h3 data-section-id=\"ynlq0v\" data-start=\"231\" data-end=\"239\">Why?</h3><p data-start=\"241\" data-end=\"279\">Take the <strong data-start=\"250\" data-end=\"278\">shortest accepted string</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"345\">While reading this string, imagine the DFA moves through states.</p><ul data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"621\"><li data-section-id=\"1xcc7mz\" data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"415\">If it visits the <strong data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"386\">same state twice</strong>, then it has gone in a loop.</li><li data-section-id=\"vqggtt\" data-start=\"416\" data-end=\"505\">That loop is unnecessary because you can remove it and still reach the accepting state.</li><li data-section-id=\"frnel1\" data-start=\"506\" data-end=\"621\">This would give an even <strong data-start=\"532\" data-end=\"559\">shorter accepted string</strong>, which is impossible since we already chose the shortest one.</li></ul><p data-start=\"623\" data-end=\"659\">So, in the shortest accepted string:</p><ul data-start=\"661\" data-end=\"869\"><li data-section-id=\"1r5k0gs\" data-start=\"661\" data-end=\"684\">No state is repeated.</li><li data-section-id=\"dphh7e\" data-start=\"685\" data-end=\"746\">Therefore, the DFA can visit at most all <strong data-start=\"728\" data-end=\"745\">5 states once</strong>.</li><li data-section-id=\"1cccgdh\" data-start=\"747\" data-end=\"869\">Visiting 5 states means only <strong data-start=\"778\" data-end=\"795\">4 transitions</strong> (edges), so the string length is at most <strong data-start=\"837\" data-end=\"842\">4</strong>, which is less than <strong data-start=\"863\" data-end=\"868\">5</strong>.</li></ul><p data-start=\"871\" data-end=\"902\">Hence, <strong data-start=\"878\" data-end=\"902\">Statement 1 is True.</strong></p><hr data-start=\"904\" data-end=\"907\"><h3 data-section-id=\"1itssl5\" data-start=\"909\" data-end=\"930\">Statement 2 (NFA)</h3><p data-start=\"932\" data-end=\"970\">Now consider an NFA with <strong data-start=\"957\" data-end=\"969\">5 states</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"972\" data-end=\"1005\">The idea is <strong data-start=\"984\" data-end=\"1004\">exactly the same</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"1007\" data-end=\"1047\">Choose the <strong data-start=\"1018\" data-end=\"1046\">shortest accepted string</strong>.</p><ul data-start=\"1049\" data-end=\"1246\"><li data-section-id=\"wciwob\" data-start=\"1049\" data-end=\"1110\">If an accepting path repeats a state, then there is a loop.</li><li data-section-id=\"1lwos2d\" data-start=\"1111\" data-end=\"1130\">Remove that loop.</li><li data-section-id=\"o7ya6t\" data-start=\"1131\" data-end=\"1178\">The NFA will still reach the accepting state.</li><li data-section-id=\"1egrcpw\" data-start=\"1179\" data-end=\"1246\">Now you have a shorter accepted string, which is a contradiction.</li></ul><p data-start=\"1248\" data-end=\"1283\">So, in the shortest accepting path:</p><ul data-start=\"1285\" data-end=\"1422\"><li data-section-id=\"gtfwok\" data-start=\"1285\" data-end=\"1304\">No state repeats.</li><li data-section-id=\"1hltuc5\" data-start=\"1305\" data-end=\"1369\">Therefore, it uses at most <strong data-start=\"1334\" data-end=\"1346\">5 states</strong> and <strong data-start=\"1351\" data-end=\"1368\">4 transitions</strong>.</li><li data-section-id=\"1fy24e6\" data-start=\"1370\" data-end=\"1422\">So the accepted string has length <strong data-start=\"1406\" data-end=\"1421\">less than 5</strong>.</li></ul><p data-start=\"1424\" data-end=\"1460\">Hence, <strong data-start=\"1431\" data-end=\"1460\">Statement 2 is also True.</strong></p><hr data-start=\"1462\" data-end=\"1465\"><h3 data-section-id=\"19wctt7\" data-start=\"1467\" data-end=\"1505\">Easy Trick to Remember (for exams)</h3><p data-start=\"1507\" data-end=\"1559\"><strong data-start=\"1510\" data-end=\"1559\">Shortest accepted string ⇒ No repeated state.</strong></p><p data-start=\"1561\" data-end=\"1565\">Why?</p><p data-start=\"1567\" data-end=\"1667\">Because <strong data-start=\"1575\" data-end=\"1618\">repeating a state means there is a loop</strong>, and removing the loop makes the string shorter.</p><p data-start=\"1669\" data-end=\"1679\">Therefore:</p><ul data-start=\"1681\" data-end=\"1835\"><li data-section-id=\"14ewg0f\" data-start=\"1681\" data-end=\"1755\"><strong data-start=\"1683\" data-end=\"1755\">DFA with n&nbsp;states → shortest accepted string has length &lt; n.</strong></li><li data-section-id=\"1agaqpg\" data-start=\"1756\" data-end=\"1835\"><strong data-start=\"1758\" data-end=\"1835\">NFA with n&nbsp;states → shortest accepted string also has length &lt; n.</strong></li></ul><p data-start=\"1837\" data-end=\"1864\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\"><strong data-start=\"1839\" data-end=\"1864\" data-is-last-node=\"\">Final Answer: C. Both</strong></p></section></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376544\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose that $\\text{L}$ is a language over a finite alphabet $\\Sigma$ with the property that for each number $\\ell \\geqslant 1$ there is some string $w$ in $\\text{L}$ with <em>length</em>$(w) \\geqslant \\ell$ such that no matter how $w$ is split up into three pieces $w=u_{1} v u_{2}$ with <em>length</em>$\\left(u_{1} v\\right) \\leqslant \\ell$ and <em>length</em>$(v) \\geqslant 1$, there is some $n \\geqslant 0$ for which $u_{1} v^{n} u_{2}$ is not in $\\text{L}$.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ is necessarily regular.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ is necessarily non-regular.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ may be regular or non-regular.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ is finite.</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 13)</small></h4><a name=\"376541\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The given property is negation of pumping lemma property/condition of regular languages. We know if a language doesn't satisfy pumping lemma then it is Non-regular. So, option B is correct.</p><hr><p>$\\color{Red}{\\text{Understand Complete Pumping Lemma, Crystal Clear:}}$</p><p>Here is&nbsp;<strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">Complete Pumping Lemma:</a>&nbsp;</strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared</a>&nbsp;</p><p>This Pumping Lemma playlist contains <strong>EVERYTHING about Pumping Lemma</strong> of Regular Languages, i.e. Proof, Examples, Variations, GATE PYQs, Finding minimum pumping length etc. Watch this playlist for Complete, Correct &amp; Clear Understanding.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376540\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose that you are given the DFA $\\text{D}$ of a regular language $\\text{L}.$ We know that $\\text{L}$ is finite and that $\\text{D}$ has $100$ states. Which of the following is a viable possibility?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ contains some string of length $100.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ contains some string of length $99.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{L}$ is empty.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The pumping length of $\\text{L}$ is $50.$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 23)</small></h4><a name=\"376537\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$L$ could be empty and pumping length is possible to be $50.$ But $L$ cannot contain a string of length $100$ or more because $L$ is finite and DFA has $100$ states. If DFA of $n$ states accepts any string of length $n-1$ or more then the language of such DFA will be infinite as we need to have some reachable loop in the DFA to accept any string of length $n-1$ or more.<br>A DFA accepts infinitely many strings iff there exists a loop in the path from initial state to any final state.</p><p><strong>Detailed Explanation of Option $A,B$ with Proof:</strong>&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ek1XsV_7QSI\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/ek1XsV_7QSI</a>&nbsp;</p><hr><p>$\\color{Red}{\\text{Understand Complete Pumping Lemma, Crystal Clear:}}$</p><p>Here is&nbsp;<strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">Complete Pumping Lemma Playlist:</a>&nbsp;</strong><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLIPZ2_p3RNHjGbysj9OvLTfL2qhsTdsbr&amp;feature=shared\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/X2VNkQBqXwo?feature=shared</a>&nbsp;</p><p>This Pumping Lemma playlist contains <strong>EVERYTHING about Pumping Lemma</strong> of Regular Languages, i.e. Proof, Examples, Variations, GATE PYQs, Finding minimum pumping length etc. Watch this playlist for Complete, Correct &amp; Clear Understanding.</p><hr><p>Useful Comment for Option B:&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376536/classes-test-series-2023-theory-computation-test-question?show=377234#c377234\" target=\"_blank\">HERE.</a></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"376967\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>A # states in DFA is 100 which means to be finite it should accept at max 98&nbsp;length string not 99 length string.</p>\n\n<p>So, Options A, and B are wrong.</p>\n\n<p>Option C is right.</p>\n\n<p>Also: the pumping length can be 50.</p>\n\n<p><strong>[x having the length as 25]<u> [y]^50 </u>[z&nbsp;having the length as 24]</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>[Edited]</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"498441\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">A. (Not Possible) Finite language requires a dead state. With 100 states, 1 is dead, leaving 99. Path for length 98 takes 99 states. So max length is 98.<br>\nB. (Not Possible) Length 99 would need 100 states for the path + 1 dead state = 101 states.<br>\nC. (Possible) Since empty language &lt; 98 , it fits.<br>\nD. (Possible) Pumping length 50 means max string length of string &lt; 50 that is &lt;= 49. Since 49 &lt; 98, it fits.<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"538625\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">The language is given to be finite. Therefore, the DFA must contain at least one sink (dead) state.<br>\n<br>\nSince the DFA has \\(100\\) states, out of which \\(1\\) is a sink state, a string of length at most \\(98\\) can be accepted.<br>\n<br>\nWe know that for a finite language, the minimum pumping length is<br>\n\\[<br>\np_{\\min} = |\\text{longest string}| + 1.<br>\n\\]<br>\n<br>\nNow, for \\(w \\in L\\),<br>\n\\[<br>\n0 \\leq |w| \\leq 98.<br>\n\\]<br>\n<br>\nTherefore, it is possible that the maximum length of a string in \\(L\\) is only \\(49\\). Hence,<br>\n\\[<br>\np_{\\min} = 49 + 1 = 50.<br>\n\\]</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376536\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following languages is/are regular?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{L}=\\{w \\mid w$ contains an equal number of occurrences of the substrings $01$ and $10\\}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The language $\\text{L}=\\{w \\mid w$ is divisible by $3$ when considered as a binary number$\\}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{L}=\\{w \\mid w$ contains an equal number of occurrences of the substrings $01$ and $11\\}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{L}=\\{w \\mid w $ contains an equal number of occurrences of the substrings $01$ and $1\\}$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 25)</small></h4><a name=\"415165\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Option B is clear as it is divisibility language contains 3 states(Remainder 0 ,remainder 1 ,remainder 2 )<br>\nOption D is when u takes 01 in the string 1 will be automatically counted like 010101 , 01--.&gt;3 times ,1--&gt;3 times likewise u can take eg<br>\nOption &nbsp;C : &nbsp;U can create infinite distinguishable set like <br>\n{ (01)^n | n&gt;=1 } &nbsp;as let (01)^2 and (01)^3 Now take 11 and append in both the strings<br>\n0101.11 ==&gt; 010111 &nbsp;==&gt; 01 =2times , 11 =2times belongs to L <br>\n01010111 &nbsp;==&gt; 01 = 3 times , 11 = 2 times not belongs to L <br>\nSO likewise every strings are distinguishable in the set so Not Regular <br>\n<br>\nOption A : Like if u see 01……01 u can’t remember 01 while not remembering 10 because anything in between 01 …..01 , 10 will be implicity counted like <br>\ntake all zeroes ,all 1’s ,etc u will find that no of times 10 is 1 less than no of times 01 so that no need to count infinite possibilities hence regular</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 23)</small></h4><a name=\"376535\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>DFA for $L$ in option A :<br>\n<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"217\" src=\"media/f4/f49cda35c149f3ccb64c.png\" width=\"624\"></strong><br>\n<br>\nLanguage in D is “every $1$ is preceded by $0$”<br>\nLanguage in C is Not regular because we can prove it by pumping lemma..take strings in which any number of $01$ can appear then same number of $11$ should appear.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"485403\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">I'm not able to understand option B.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"507065\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>BY THE WAY question must mention that what is alphabet for a language<br>lets say for option A, L={w∣w&nbsp;contains an equal number of occurrences of the substrings&nbsp;10&nbsp;and&nbsp;10}.<br>L can be regular or non - regular for different alphabets&nbsp;<br>here i am giving example for L is non regular if L is over {0,1,2}<br>consider a this infinte set S&nbsp; {(012)^n | n&gt;0} now this set S contains infinite strings<br>by pigein hole principle as FA have finite states and we have infinite strings so, we can say that atleast two string must end on one state.&nbsp;</p><p>Let the two strings be w1 and w2 such that x !=y</p><p>w1 = (012)^y this string contain y substring 01&nbsp;<br>w2 = (012)^x this string contain x substring 01</p><p>let the state they end up on be s1<br>now if we read (102)^x from s1, there is contradiction of whether to accept string or not because<br>if accept this string from s1 then we will also accept (012)^y(102)^x<br>or if reject this string from s1 then we will also reject (012)^x(102)^x</p><p>so by MYHILL NERODE theroem, we have given an infite set of distinguishable for this L over {0,1,2},&nbsp;<strong>THUS L MUST BE NON-REGULAR</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376534\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":15,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Let $\\mathrm{D}$ be some DFA (Deterministic Finite Automata) with $10$ states and let the cardinality of the input alphabet set be $3.$ If the language accepted by $\\mathrm{D}$ i.e., $\\mathrm{L}(\\mathrm{D})$ is finite, the maximum value of $|\\mathrm{L}(\\mathrm{D})|$ will be _______, $(|\\mathrm{L}(\\mathrm{D})|$ denotes the cardinality of $\\mathrm{L}(\\mathrm{D}))$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[9841,9841],"answerText":"9841","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 57)</small></h4><a name=\"376527\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer $: 9841$</p>\n\n<p>$\\color{red}{\\text{Detailed Video Solution, with Complete Analysis:}}$&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/kPLciwgwKaE\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/kPLciwgwKaE</a>&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\n<strong>Since the language is Finite, We cannot have loops in the way from the initial state to the final state.</strong></p>\n\n<p>And Since we need to maximize the cardinality of $L(D)$, so, we need as&nbsp;many final states as possible&nbsp;while preserving the finiteness of the $L(D)$.<br>\nSo, the DFA will look like this :<br>\n<br>\n<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8b440e31bd0b2cc162a8.png\"><br>\n<br>\nWhere all the states except the last one are final states, and the last state is a non-final trap state.</p>\n\n<p>The last state $q_L$ is a&nbsp;trap state (i.e. $\\delta(q_L, a) = q_L, \\forall a \\in \\Sigma$ ) and non-final as well.<br>\n<br>\nSo, $\\operatorname{\\max}(|L(D)|)=\\Sigma^{0}+\\Sigma^{1}+\\Sigma^{2} \\ldots \\ldots+\\Sigma^{n-2}$<br>\n<br>\nHence, the answer is $9841.$</p>\n\n<p><strong>Why is the last state a trap state??</strong></p>\n\n<p>Because if it is not a trap state, then you need to make a transition from last state to some other state &amp; if you do so, then a loop will occur which makes language infinite.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<hr>\n<p>NOTE: The following Video Solution covers the Variation <strong>in case of NFA</strong> as well.</p>\n\n<p>$\\color{red}{\\text{Detailed Video Solution, with Complete Analysis:}}$&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/kPLciwgwKaE\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">https://youtu.be/kPLciwgwKaE</a>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 24)</small></h4><a name=\"486633\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><span style=\"text-decoration: line-through;\"><img alt=\"Image not available\" width=\"683\" height=\"900\" src=\"media/7e/7e64bf7e390677b27e4b.jpg\"></span></p><p>This way I approach the question , I hope it will help you .&nbsp;</p><p><br>Thank You !&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"531916\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Answer: 9841</b></p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\"><b data-path-to-node=\"1\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Explanation:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"2\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Preventing Cycles:</b> For the DFA to accept a finite language, it cannot have cycles. It must transition linearly and end in a single \"dead state\" (trap state) that loops on itself.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Available States:</b> Out of the 10 states, 1 is the dead state, leaving 9 sequential states ($q_0$ to $q_8$).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,2,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Maximizing Paths:</b> To maximize combinations, make all 9 sequential states <i data-path-to-node=\"2,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"73\">accepting states</i>. Use all 3 input symbols to transition from state $q_i$ directly to $q_{i+1}$.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,3,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Geometric Growth:</b> The number of unique strings accepted at each state grows by powers of the alphabet size ($3$):</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"2,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,3,1,0,0\">Strings at $q_0$: $3^0$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,3,1,1,0\">Strings at $q_1$: $3^1$</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,3,1,2,0\">...</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,3,1,3,0\">Strings at $q_8$: $3^8$</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"2,4,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"2,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Calculation:</b> The total maximum cardinality is the sum of this geometric progression:</p><p>$$|L(D)| = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + \\dots + 3^8$$</p><p>$$|L(D)| = \\frac{3^9 - 1}{3 - 1} = \\frac{19683 - 1}{2} = 9841$$</p></li></ul></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376526\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":25.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":15}],"stats":{"questions":15,"maxMarks":19,"types":{"MSQ":5,"MCQ":6,"NAT":4},"withoutKey":4}};
